--- title
# Kaduo

--- body
Live video production in the browser, integrated with the commercial activity taking place around a broadcast.

--- actions
[Product website](https://kaduo.shop/) · [All products](/en/#products)
:::

::: section topic-1
## The studio

A live broadcast begins with the device's camera and microphone. The production interface handles preview, framing and visual elements that must appear in the transmitted image: captions, product information, promotional graphics and selected audience interactions.

The key distinction is between **drawing over a video player** and **composing the video itself**. Kaduo uses browser media capabilities to produce a composed outgoing stream; supported graphics are therefore part of the broadcast image.
:::

::: section topic-2
## Media and commerce

The media path has different requirements from the shopping path. Video needs continuous transport, timing and adaptive handling of network conditions; catalog, orders and promotional information need transactional consistency. Mixing those responsibilities would make both harder to change.

The system also covers recorded short-form video and audience interaction during live sessions. The studio can be used from a camera-equipped device without requiring a separate desktop production application.
:::

::: section topic-3
## The engineering trade-off

Browser-side composition keeps production close to the camera and makes visual adjustments immediate. It also makes rendering dependent on browser capabilities and the device's available processing power. A server-side compositor would offer a more uniform rendering environment, at the cost of additional processing infrastructure.

Current engineering work focuses on separating device selection, capture configuration, composed-media assembly and transmission monitoring. Those boundaries can be tested independently; broadcast reliability still requires testing on physical devices and real networks.
:::

::: section topic-4
## Verification

Isolated regression tests cover selected media-processing rules, input selection and stream composition. A production frontend build is checked in CI. Those checks do not establish end-to-end latency or the number of viewers a deployment can sustain. Those claims require instrumented broadcasts and measurements under defined network conditions.
:::

